# `$hasContiguousIndex(list, i)` and `$getOwnContiguousIndex(list, i)` say "the list has an element of its own at i in a plain vector" and "that element". Their emitted forms know the two storages of boxed
# values, so a list of fractions failed the test at its first element and went the general way: forEachFrom by call, the arrow by value, `i in list`, `list[i]`: three times the instructions of plain Bun.
# Behind a row that has shown the storage of numbers, the pair is the walk's own pair for such storage, and the arrow gets a raw number.
# Link has seen no whole number arrive at this arrow: the storage of whole numbers is not admitted, and the kind is not asked again in the turns.
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-NOT: = HasArrayStorage {{*}}arrayStorage=Double [admitsInt32Storage]
# CHECK: = HasArrayStorage {{*}}arrayStorage=Double
# CHECK: [[test]]: boolean @Boolean = IsElementPresent [[list]], [[at]], {{*}}arrayStorage=Double
# CHECK: : number @Double = GetArrayElement [[list]], [[at]], {{*}}arrayStorage=Double level=Proven {{*}}isPresent
# No read of boxed values stands behind a test that was answered for numbers: it would hand out the bits of a double as a value.
# RUN(PAIRS): walk f1 mechanisms=all
# PAIRS: = IsElementPresent
# PAIRS-NOT: = GetOwnContiguousIndex
# PAIRS: = GetArrayElement {{*}}isPresent
# PAIRS: = IsElementPresent
# PAIRS-NOT: = GetOwnContiguousIndex
# PAIRS: = GetArrayElement {{*}}isPresent
# Whole numbers and fractions arrive at this one: the row admits either storage of numbers.
# RUN(BOTH): walk f2 mechanisms=all
